## Interface: 30300
## Title: COA Dungeon Run
## Notes: v0.24.4 - capture, display and CURATION. A dungeon route IS a sequence of pulls, so record the pulls: combat start and end are the markers, the path between them is sampled once a second out of combat, and both entrances are captured automatically. Records everything and filters nothing - judgement happens offline against the whole set. Also records the instance identity and DIFFICULTY at arrival, which bosses the route ENGAGED (never a roster, never a fraction), and who killed you at a terminal stop - read from the client's own DeathRecap rather than a second combat-log listener. The map draws a run back onto the client's own tiles and never learns a dungeon; point facts live on the MAP as a tooltip; the companion is the CURATION pane - load, rename, comment, delete, filter by kind, and slice by TIME with an envelope, a window, play and skip. Curation only ever changes what you SEE: nothing it does touches the record. Also a CUSTOM PIN: where the client emits no event, the player is the sensor - /dr pin drops a point and promotion gives it meaning later. The map now holds LAYERS rather than a run - a run slot and a route slot, loaded independently - because authoring a route means looking at the evidence it came from. And PROMOTION is the third surface: select a node, mint a beacon into a route or a personal note onto your own plane - place carries over, the event does not, and the meaning is edited in afterwards. And a prototype DRIVER consumes them: arm a route in its own dungeon, scan for the current stage only, say when you reach it, advance - built before the behaviours are defined because a consumer is how they declare themselves. A beacon now carries the OUTCOME OF ITS SATISFACTION - advance by one, or go to a stage you typed - which is the whole of what a checkpoint is: index = max(index, outcome), ratcheted so nothing can ever walk it backwards, and the promoter lists the running order sorted by stage value so bad numbering is visible rather than refused. The stage itself is set at the mint through a ghosted field - it shows the next free round number and walks gaps, and you can type your own, including a 4.1 between 4 and 5, because inserting detail should never renumber anything - and the stage stays editable afterwards, with a match count that shows a collision as you type it and a light readout of which numbers are still free inside the route. The map ZOOMS and PANS, 1x to 4x, anchored on the view centre rather than the cursor - because the cursor is also the pen, and drawing tools are unusable on the coarser maps where a 5-yard radius is under two pixels. Its controls are a widget of their own: a stage button that cycles 100/125/150/200 and takes you there, a pan pad that moves a quarter view per press, recentre and reset - and mouse-wheel zoom and right-click panning are TICKS that default OFF, because the wheel belongs to the world camera and right-drag to camera-look, and an addon should not take either without being asked. /dr
